  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warming trend will bring above average high\ntemperatures by mid to late week, along with dry conditions.\n\nLatest analysis reveals weakening low pressure (~1010 mb) \ncentered near Cape Cod; the remnants of this past weekend's \nNor'easter. While the system still maintains a relatively \nimpressive appearance in early morning IR/WV satellite imagery, \nit continues to weaken rapidly as it accelerates off the New \nEngland coast early this morning. A few showers even developed \nnorth of the RIC metro late last night in association with a \nweak shortwave rotating through the base of the exiting upper \nlevel trough. In the wake of this system, gradual clearing is \nexpected late today and especially tonight, as broad 1018+ mb \nhigh pressure centered over the OH/TN River Valleys gradually \nbuilds east through midweek. Meanwhile, upper ridging will \nrebuild over the western Atlantic into the SE CONUS. The \nresultant height rises support a gradual warm-up through the end\nof the work week. That trend begins today, with pleasant \nweather expected. Partly to mostly cloudy conditions this \nmorning will give way to clearing this afternoon and evening. \nHigh temperatures will reach well into the 70s area- wide, with\nreadings near 80 inland. Clearing could set the stage for some\nareas of fog and low stratus to develop late tonight. Lows\nmainly in the 50s inland to low-mid 60s at the coast.\n\nPartly to mostly sunny, with highs to climb well into the 80s \naway from the immediate coast tomorrow, with unseasonably warm \ntemperatures likely Thursday and Friday. A few locations could \nflirt with the lower 90s on these days, with the best chances \nFriday as compressional heating maximizes ahead of an \napproaching cold front.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An unsettled weather pattern with cooler and \nwetter conditions appears likely for this weekend into early \nnext week.\n\nAn increasingly unsettled pattern looks to take shape for the\nweekend into early next week. A deep closed low currently over \nthe Desert Southwest into the Intermountain West looks to open \nup by midweek as it absorbs the mid-level remnants of TC Polo \nand lifts across the central and southern Plains. This system \nwill eventually become absorbed into a northern stream trough \ndigging across the interior Northeast into the northern Mid- \nAtlantic over the weekend. This will serve to dampen the \nSoutheast ridge a bit and allow an influx of Gulf and Pacific \nmoisture across the Gulf Coast and Mid-South, potentially \nreaching our region over the weekend. There is still \nconsiderable spread among the probabilistic model guidance \nregarding the exact timing and track of these features, but a \ncold front is likely to approach the area over the weekend into \nearly next week. Ahead of this front and its attendant surface \nlow/wave, rain chances look to increase late Saturday and \nespecially Sunday/Monday. One signal common to all guidance, \neven this far out, is a notable increase in PWATs, with values \npotentially reaching 1.5-2\" by Sunday. With the front potentially\nsettling slowly across the region, with the potential for \nlocally heavy rainfall also potentially increasing once again \nlate in the weekend. There is still plenty of time to monitor \nthis potential over the next several days. In terms of \ntemperatures, increased clouds/precip and onshore flow favor \ncooler temps over the weekend into early next week, but not \nabnormally so. Highs Saturday likely range through the 70s and \n80s, with higher PoPs and cloud cover bringing slightly cooler \n70s Sunday and Monday into the middle of next week.",
